==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|estanson}}, {{m|fro|estanchon}}, (Modern French {{m|fr|étançon}}), from {{m|fro|estance||a stay, a prop}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|stans||standing}}, present participle of {{m|la|stō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|stănʹshən}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈstænʃən/}} ''or''
* {{rfv-pron|en}} {{enPR|stänʹshən}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈstɑːnʃən/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}
[[File:Stanchions and velvet rope - 37th G8 summit in Deauville 002.jpg|thumb|Stanchions supporting velvet rope]]

# A [[vertical]] [[pole]], [[post]], or [[support]].
#* '''1938''', [[w:Xavier Herbert|Xavier Herbert]], ''[[w:Capricornia (novel)|Capricornia]]'', New York: D. Appleton-Century, 1943, Chapter IX, p. 149, [https://archive.org/details/capricornianovel00herb]
#*: The train began to move. Lace walked with it, holding a '''stanchion'''.
#* '''2013''', [[w:J. M. Coetzee|J. M. Coetzee]], ''[[w:The Childhood of Jesus|The Childhood of Jesus]]''. Melbourne, Australia: The Text Publishing Company. chapter 27. p. 268.
#*: He staggers against a '''stanchion''', trips over a rope, and tumbles into the space between the quay and the steel plates of the freighter.
# A [[framework]] of such posts, used to [[secure]] or [[confine]] [[cattle]].

